\rem WORDTABLE OET-LV_DC_word_table.tsv
\h 1 Ezra
\toc1 The First Book of Ezra
\toc2 1 Ezra
\toc3 1Es
\mt1 The First Book of Ezra
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk The First Book of Ezra\bk*, also known as \bk 1 Esdras\bk*, recounts the fall of Yehudah (Judah) and the return of exiles to Yerushalem (Jerusalem). It begins with King Yoshiyah's (Josiah's) Passover celebration, follows the disasters that lead to exile in Bavel (Babylon), and then describes the return, the rebuilding of the temple, and the work of Ezra. Much of this will be familiar to readers of \bk The Chronicles\bk*, \bk Ezra\bk*, and \bk Nehemyah\bk*, although the material is arranged differently.
\ip A distinctive part of this account is the debate between three of King Dareyavesh's (Darius') guards about what is strongest. One argues for wine, another for the king, and Zorobabel argues for women before declaring that truth is stronger than everything. His success leads to royal support for the return to Yerushalem and the rebuilding of the temple.
\ip The account emphasises restored worship, support for the temple, and the need to live according to God's instructions. It ends with Ezra reading and explaining the law to the assembled people, who respond with worship and celebration.
\ip \bk The First Book of Ezra\bk* is recognised as Deuterocanonical Scripture by the Greek Orthodox and Russian Orthodox Churches. It is not recognised by the Romaios Catholic Church, but 1 Ezra is placed as an appendix to the Latin Vulgate Bible.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Yoshiyah's Passover and the fall of Yehudah \ior 1:1-58\ior*
\io1 Permission to return and opposition to rebuilding \ior 2:1-30\ior*
\io1 The guards' debate and Zorobabel's request \ior 3:1–4:63\ior*
\io1 The returning families and the rebuilding of the temple \ior 5:1–7:15\ior*
\io1 Ezra's return and the response to marriages with foreigners \ior 8:1–9:36\ior*
\io1 Ezra reads God's law to the people \ior 9:37-55\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Yoshiyah celebrates the Passover
\p
\v 1 Yoshiyah \add !(Josiah)\add* held the¦105537 Passover¦105538 in¦105539 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* to his master, and offered the¦105546 Passover¦105547 the¦105548 fourteenth¦105549 day¦105550 of¦105551 the¦105553 first¦105554 month,\x + \xo 1:1: \xt 2Ki 23:21; 2Ch 35:1.\x*
\v 2 having set the¦105556 priests¦105557 according¦105558 to¦105558 their daily courses, being arrayed in their vestments, in the¦105562 master's temple¦105563.
\v 3 He spoke to the Levites, Yisrael's \add !(Israel)\add* temple servants,\x + \xo 1:3: \xt Num 3:9.\x* that they should make themselves holy to the master, to set the holy ark of the master in the house that King Shelomoh \add !(Solomon)\add* the son of David had built.
\v 4 He said, “You no longer need to carry it on your shoulders. Now therefore serve the master your god, and minister to his people Yisrael, and prepare yourselves by your fathers’ houses and kindred,
\v 5 according to the writing of King David of Yisrael, and according to the magnificence of Shelomoh his son. Stand in the holy place according to the divisions of your Levite families who minister in the presence of your kindred the descendants of Yisrael.
\v 6 Offer the Passover in order, prepare the sacrifices for your kindred, and keep the Passover according to the master's commandment, which was given to Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add*.
\p
\v 7 To¦105685 the¦105687 people¦105688 which were present, Yoshiyah gave thirty¦105694 thousand lambs and kids, and three thousand calves. These things were given from¦105699 the¦105700 king's possessions, as he¦105698 promised¦105704, to the¦105705 people¦105706 and¦105707 to the¦105708 priests¦105709 and¦105710 Levites.
\v 8 Hilkiyah, Zekaryah \add !(Zechariah)\add*, and Yehiel,\f + \fr 1:8 \fq Jehiel, \ft 2 Chronicles 35:8.\f* the¦105719 rulers of¦105721 the¦105721 temple¦105722, gave¦105713 to¦105720 the¦105723 priests¦105724 for the Passover¦105726 two thousand and six¦105729 hundred sheep¦105727, and three hundred¦105729 calves.
\v 9 Konaniyah, Shemayah, Netanel his brother¦105739, Hashavyah, Yeiel, and Yoram¦105745 \add !(Joram)\add*, captains over thousands, gave¦105747 to¦105747 the Levites for the Passover¦105751 five thousand sheep¦105752 and seven hundred calves.
\p
\v 10 When these things were done, the priests and Levites, having the unleavened bread, stood in proper order according to the kindred,
\v 11 and according to the several divisions by fathers’ houses, before the people, to offer to the master as it's written in the book of Mosheh. They did this in the morning.
\v 12 They roasted the¦105797 Passover¦105798 lamb with fire¦105799, as required. They boiled the¦105803 sacrifices¦105804 in¦105806 the¦105807 brazen vessels and¦105809 cauldrons with¦105811 a pleasing smell,
\v 13 and¦105813 set them before all¦105815 the¦105816 people¦105819. Afterward¦105820 they prepared¦105823 for themselves and for their kindred the¦105827 priests¦105828, the sons¦105831 of Aharon \add !(Aaron)\add*.
\v 14 Because the¦105833 priests¦105835 offered the¦105837 fat until¦105839 night. The¦105842 Levites prepared¦105844 for themselves and¦105846 for their kindred the¦105847 priests¦105848, the sons¦105851 of Aharon.
\v 15 The holy singers also¦105853, the¦105854 sons¦105856 of Asaf¦105857, were¦105858 in their order, according¦105863 to¦105863 the¦105864 appointment of David: Asaf¦105869, Zekaryah, and Yedutun, who¦105874 represented the¦105876 king¦105877.
\v 16 Moreover the¦105879 gatekeepers were at every gate¦105883. No¦105884 one needed to depart from his daily duties, for¦105892 their kindred the¦105895 Levites prepared¦105897 for them.
\p
\v 17 So¦105899 the¦105901 things that belonged to the¦105902 master's sacrifices¦105903 were accomplished in¦105906 that¦105907 day¦105909, in holding the¦105911 Passover¦105912,
\v 18 and¦105913 offering sacrifices¦105916 on¦105917 the¦105918 altar¦105921 of¦105919 the¦105919 master, according¦105922 to¦105922 King¦105926 Yoshiyah's commandment.
\v 19 So¦105928 the¦105930 children¦105931 of Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* which were present at that time¦105937 held the¦105939 Passover¦105940 and¦105941 the feast of unleavened¦105945 bread seven¦105947 days¦105946.
\v 20 Such¦105953 a Passover¦105952 hadn't been held in¦105954 Yisrael since the¦105958 time¦105959 of¦105961 the¦105961 prophet¦105962 Shmuel \add !(Samuel)\add*.
\v 21 Indeed, none of Yisrael's kings¦105966 held such a Passover¦105971 as Yoshiyah with the¦105977 priests¦105978, the¦105980 Levites, and¦105982 the¦105983 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, held with all¦105986 Yisrael that were present in their dwelling place at Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 22 This¦106001 Passover¦106002 was held in the eighteenth year¦105997 of Yoshiyah's reign.
\v 23 Yoshiyah's works¦106007 were upright before¦106009 his master with a heart¦106014 full of godliness.
\v 24 Moreover the¦106018 things that happened in his days have been written in times¦106026 past, about¦106027 those who¦106028 sinned and¦106030 did wickedly against the¦106033 master more than¦106035 any other people or kingdom¦106039, and¦106040 how they grieved him greatly,\f + \fr 1:24 \ft Or, \fqa sensibly \ft Judges 16:17.\f* so that the¦106046 master's words¦106047 were confirmed against¦106051 Yisrael.
\s1 Yoshiyah is killed in battle
\p
\v 25 Now after¦106054 all¦106055 these acts of Yoshiyah \add !(Josiah)\add*, it¦106058 happened¦106060 that Par'oh \add !(Pharaoh)\add* the king¦106062 of Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add* came to¦106064 make war¦106065 at Karkemish on¦106067 the¦106070 Perat \add !(Euphrates)\add*; and¦106072 Yoshiyah went¦106073 out¦106073 against him.\x + \xo 1:25: \xt 2Ch 35:20.\x*
\v 26 But¦106078 the king¦106082 of Mitsrayim sent to¦106084 him, saying, “What do I¦106086 have to do with you¦106088, Oh king¦106090 of Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add*?
\v 27 I wasn't sent out from the¦106099 master God¦106100 against¦106101 you, for¦106102 my war¦106106 is¦106108 against the Perat. Now¦106110 the master is with¦106112 me, yes, the master is¦106114 with¦106117 me hastening me forward. Depart from me, and¦106122 don't¦106123 be against the¦106125 master.”
\p
\v 28 However, Yoshiyah didn't turn back to¦106132 his chariot¦106134, but¦106136 tried to fight with him, not regarding the words of the prophet¦106144 Yirmiyah \add !(Jeremiah)\add* from¦106145 the master's mouth¦106146,
\v 29 but¦106148 joined battle¦106152 with¦106150 him in¦106153 the¦106154 plain of Megidon, and¦106157 the commanders came¦106158 down¦106158 against King¦106163 Yoshiyah.
\v 30 Then¦106165 the¦106167 king¦106168 said¦106166 to his servants, “Carry me away out of the¦106169 battle, for I'm¦106177 very weak¦106177!” Immediately¦106181 his servants carried him away¦106187 out of the¦106188 army.
\v 31 Then¦106190 he got into his second chariot¦106194. After he¦106197 was brought back to Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* he¦106205 died, and¦106206 was buried¦106207 in¦106208 the¦106209 tomb of his ancestors.
\v 32 All¦106214 Yehudah mourned for Yoshiyah. Yirmiyah the¦106223 prophet¦106224 lamented for¦106225 Yoshiyah, and the¦106228 chief men with¦106230 the women¦106231 made lamentation for him to this¦106235 day¦106236. This was given out for an ordinance to¦106241 be¦106241 done continually¦106242 in all¦106244 the¦106245 nation of Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*.
\v 33 These things are written in¦106251 the¦106252 book¦106253 of the¦106254 histories of Yehudah's kings¦106258, and¦106261 every one¦106264 of the¦106266 acts that Yoshiyah did, and¦106269 his glory¦106271, and¦106273 his understanding¦106275 in¦106277 the¦106281 master's law¦106279, and¦106282 the things that he'd¦106285 done before, and¦106286 the¦106287 things now¦106288 told, are reported in¦106290 the¦106291 book¦106292 of the¦106293 kings¦106294 of Yisrael and¦106296 Yehudah.
\s1 Yehudah's last kings and the fall of Yerushalem
\p
\v 34 The people took¦106299 Yehoahaz\f + \fr 1:34 \ft Another reading is, \fqa Jeconias.\f* the son¦106306 of Yoshiyah, and made him king¦106309 instead of¦106312 Yoshiyah his father¦106313, when he¦106314 was¦106315 twenty-three years¦106316 old.\x + \xo 1:34: \xt 2Ki 23:30; 2Ch 36:1.\x*
\v 35 He reigned¦106320 in¦106321 Yehudah and¦106323 Yerushalem for three¦106326 months¦106325. Then¦106327 the king¦106330 of¦106332 Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add* deposed him from reigning¦106334 in¦106335 Yerushalem.
\v 36 He set a tax on the¦106339 people of one hundred¦106343 talents of silver and¦106344 one talent of gold¦106345.
\v 37 The king¦106350 of Mitsrayim also made King¦106352 Yehoyakim his brother¦106355 king¦106357 of Yehudah and¦106360 Yerushalem.
\v 38 And¦106362 Yehoyakim imprisoned the¦106365 nobles and¦106368 apprehended his brother¦106370 Zarakes, and brought him up out¦106374 of Mitsrayim.
\p
\v 39 Yehoyakim was¦106378 twenty-five¦106379 years¦106376 old when¦106381 he began to¦106382 reign¦106382 in Yehudah and Yerushalem. He did¦106388 that¦106389 which was evil¦106390 in the sight of the master.\x + \xo 1:39: \xt 2Ch 36:4-5.\x*
\v 40 King¦106399 Nevukadnetsar \add !(Nebuchadnezzar)\add* of Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add* came up against him, bound¦106402 him with a chain of brass, and¦106407 carried him to Bavel.
\v 41 Nevukadnetsar also¦106411 took¦106418 some of¦106416 the¦106416 master's holy vessels, carried them away¦106412, and¦106420 stored them in¦106423 his own temple¦106425 at Bavel.
\v 42 But¦106430 those things that are reported of him, and¦106434 of his uncleanness and¦106438 impiety, are written in¦106441 the records of the kings¦106447.
\v 43 Then¦106448 Yehoyakin his son¦106454 reigned¦106449 in his place. When¦106456 he¦106451 was made king, he¦106455 was¦106459 eighteen years¦106460 old.
\v 44 He reigned¦106462 three¦106465 months¦106464 and¦106466 ten¦106468 days¦106467 in¦106469 Yerushalem. He did¦106472 that which was evil¦106474 before¦106475 the master.
\p
\v 45 So after¦106478 a year¦106479 Nevukadnetsar sent¦106480 and caused him to be brought to Bavel with the¦106487 holy vessels¦106489 of¦106490 the¦106490 master,
\v 46 and¦106492 made Tsidkiyah \add !(Zedekiah)\add* king¦106495 of Yehudah and¦106498 Yerushalem when he was¦106500 twenty-one years¦106501 old. He reigned¦106504 eleven¦106507 years.
\v 47 He also¦106508 did¦106509 that¦106510 which was evil¦106511 in the sight of the master, and¦106514 didn't heed the¦106518 words¦106520 that were spoken by¦106521 Yirmiyah the¦106523 prophet¦106524 from¦106525 the master's mouth¦106526.
\v 48 After King¦106533 Nevukadnetsar had made him to swear by the¦106535 master's name¦106536, he broke his oath and rebelled. Hardening his neck¦106544 and¦106545 his heart¦106547, he¦106548 transgressed¦106549 the laws of the master, the god¦106553 of Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*.
\v 49 Moreover the¦106556 governors¦106557 of¦106559 the¦106559 people¦106560 and¦106561 of the¦106562 priests¦106563 did many things wickedly, exceeding all¦106568 the¦106569 defilements of all¦106571 nations¦106573, and¦106574 defiled the¦106576 master's temple¦106577, which was sanctified in¦106582 Yerushalem.
\v 50 Their ancestors' god sent¦106585 by his messenger to¦106595 call¦106595 them back, because¦106591 he had compassion on them and¦106600 on his dwelling place.
\v 51 But¦106605 they mocked his messengers. In¦106607 the¦106608 day¦106613 when the¦106618 master spoke¦106614, they scoffed at his prophets¦106619
\v 52 until¦106621 he¦106624, being angry with his people for their great ungodliness, commanded to¦106625 bring up the¦106626 kings¦106637 of¦106629 the¦106630 Kasdim \add !(Chaldeans)\add* against¦106634 them.
\v 53 They killed¦106641 their young men with the¦106642 sword¦106646 around their holy¦106649 temple¦106651, and spared neither young man or young¦106657 woman¦106657, old man or child; but¦106662 he delivered¦106664 all¦106663 of them into¦106664 their hands¦106667.
\v 54 They took¦106690 all¦106670 the¦106671 holy vessels¦106673 of¦106674 the master, both great¦106677 and¦106678 small¦106680, with the¦106682 treasure chests of¦106684 the¦106684 master's ark and¦106686 the¦106687 king's treasures, and carried¦106691 them away to Bavel.
\v 55 They burnt the¦106696 master's house¦106697, broke down Yerushalem's walls¦106703, and¦106705 burnt its towers with fire¦106711.
\v 56 As for her glorious things, they didn't stop until they'd brought them all¦106714 to nothing. He¦106717 carried the people who weren't slain with¦106723 the sword to Bavel.
\v 57 They were¦106728 servants to him and¦106731 to his children¦106733 until¦106735 the Persians reigned¦106737, to¦106737 fulfil the¦106742 master's word by Yirmiyah's mouth¦106745:
\v 58 “Until¦106747 the land¦106751 has enjoyed its Sabbaths, the¦106756 whole¦106755 time¦106757 of her desolation will she¦106760 keep Sabbath¦106753, to fulfil seventy¦106765 years¦106764.
\c 2
\s1 Koresh allows the exiles to return
\p
\v 1 In the first¦106770 year of King Koresh \add !(Cyrus)\add* of the Persians, that the master's word by Yirmiyah's \add !(Jeremiah)\add* mouth¦106776 might be accomplished,\x + \xo 2:1: \xt 2Ch 36:22,23; Ezr 1:1.\x*
\v 2 the master stirred up the¦106780 spirit¦106781 of King¦106783 Koresh of the Persians, and¦106785 he made an \add official\add* announcement throughout all¦106788 his kingdom¦106790, and¦106792 also¦106792 by writing,
\v 3 saying, “Koresh king of the¦106806 Persians says¦106798: The¦106808 master of¦106810 Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*, the¦106810 Most High master, has made me king of the¦106813 whole world¦106807,
\v 4 and¦106815 commanded me to¦106818 build¦106818 him a house¦106820 at Yerushalem that's¦106823 in¦106824 Yehudah.
\v 5 If¦106827 therefore¦106830 there are any of you¦106831 that are of¦106833 his people, let the¦106833 master, even his master, be¦106836 with¦106840 him, and¦106842 let him go up to¦106843 Yerushalem that's in¦106848 Yehudah, and build the¦106852 house¦106853 of¦106854 the¦106854 master of¦106856 Yisrael. He's the master who lives in¦106863 Yerushalem.
\v 6 Therefore¦106866, of those who¦106868 live in various places¦106869, let those who¦106873 are in¦106874 his own place¦106876 help each one with gold¦106879, with silver,
\v 7 with gifts, with horses¦106886, and¦106887 cattle, beside the other things which have been added by vow for the master's temple¦106898 which is in¦106902 Yerushalem.
\p
\v 8 Then¦106904 the chief of the families of Yehudah \add !(Judah)\add* and of the tribe¦106914 of Benyamin stood up, with the¦106916 priests¦106917, the¦106919 Levites, and¦106921 all¦106922 whose spirit¦106927 the master had stirred to go up, to¦106929 build¦106929 the house¦106930 for the master which¦106923 is in¦106934 Yerushalem.
\v 9 Those who¦106937 lived around them helped them in¦106941 all¦106942 things with silver and¦106945 gold¦106946, with horses¦106947 and cattle, and with very¦106953 many gifts that were vowed by a great number whose minds¦106956 were so moved.
\p
\v 10 King¦106960 Koresh also brought¦106962 out¦106962 the¦106963 holy vessels¦106965 of¦106966 the¦106966 master, which¦106968 Nevukadnetsar \add !(Nebuchadnezzar)\add* had carried away from¦106971 Yerushalem and¦106973 had stored in¦106976 his temple¦106978 of idols¦106978.
\v 11 Now when¦106981 King¦106985 Koresh of the Persians had brought them out, he delivered them to¦106987 Mitredat his treasurer,
\v 12 and by him they were delivered¦106987 to Sheshebatsar\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Another reading is, \fqa Simanassar.\f* Yehudah's governor.
\v 13 This was the number¦107004 of them: one thousand gold¦107007 cups, one thousand¦107008 silver¦107010 cups, twenty-nine¦107014 silver censers, thirty¦107017 gold bowls, two thousand and four hundred and ten¦107021 silver bowls, and¦107022 one thousand¦107025 other vessels¦107024.
\v 14 So¦107027 all¦107028 the vessels¦107029 of gold¦107031 and¦107032 of silver¦107033 were brought up, even five thousand and four¦107035 hundred¦107035 and seventy-nine,
\v 15 and were carried back by¦107039 Sheshebatsar, together¦107041 with the returning exiles, from Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add* to Yerushalem.
\s1 Opposition stops the rebuilding of Yerushalem
\p
\v 16 In¦107050 the time¦107058 of King¦107057 Artahshashta \add !(Artaxerxes)\add* of the Persians, Bishlam, Mitredat, Teve'el, Rehum,\f + \fr 2:16 \fqa Rehum,\f* Rehum, and Shimshay\f + \fr 2:16 \fqa Shimshai.\f* the¦107080 scribe¦107081, with their other associates, dwelling¦107063 in¦107064 Shomron \add !(Samaria)\add* and¦107082 other places¦107095, wrote to him against those who lived in¦107090 Yehudah and¦107092 Yerushalem the following letter:\x + \xo 2:16: \xt Ezr 4:7.\x*
\p
\v 17 “To King¦107099 Artahshashta our master, from your servants, Rehum the recorder, Shimshay the¦107111 scribe¦107112, and¦107113 the rest of their council, and the judges¦107120 who¦107121 are in¦107122 Coele-Syria and¦107125 Foinikai:
\v 18 Let it now¦107128 be¦107130 known to our lord¦107132 the king¦107133, that¦107134 the¦107135 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* that have come up from¦107138 you¦107139 to us, having come to Yerushalem, are building that rebellious and wicked city¦107146, and are repairing its marketplaces¦107154 and¦107156 walls¦107158, and¦107160 are laying the foundation of a temple¦107161.
\v 19 Now if¦107163 this¦107165 city¦107166 is built¦107168 and¦107169 its walls¦107171 are finished, they won't¦107175 only refuse to¦107177 give¦107177 tribute, but¦107178 will even stand up against kings¦107180.
\v 20 Since¦107183 the¦107185 things pertaining to¦107186 the¦107187 temple¦107188 are now in hand, we think it appropriate not¦107192 to neglect such¦107195 a matter,
\v 21 but¦107196 to speak to our lord¦107199 the king¦107200, to the intent that¦107201, if¦107202 it's your pleasure, search may be made in¦107206 the books of your ancestors.
\v 22 You'll find¦107214 in¦107215 the¦107216 records what's written¦107218 about¦107219 these things, and¦107221 will understand¦107222 that¦107223 that¦107224 city¦107225 was¦107227 rebellious, troubling both kings¦107230 and¦107231 cities¦107232,
\v 23 and¦107234 that the¦107235 Yehudim were rebellious, and¦107238 kept starting wars there in¦107241 the past. Because¦107246 this cause¦107248, this¦107250 city¦107251 was laid¦107253 waste¦107253.
\v 24 Therefore now¦107254 we do declare to you¦107257, Oh lord¦107258 the king¦107259, that¦107260 if¦107261 this¦107262 city¦107263 is built¦107265 again, and¦107266 its walls¦107269 set up again, you'll from then on have no¦107272 passage into¦107275 Coele-Syria and¦107279 Foinikai.”
\p
\v 25 Then¦107281 the¦107283 king¦107284 wrote back again to Rehum the¦107286 recorder, Rehum, Shimshay the¦107288 scribe, and to the¦107296 rest that of their associates who¦107298 lived in¦107302 Shomron \add !(Samaria)\add*, Aram \add !(Syria)\add*, and Foinikai, as follows:
\p
\v 26 “I've read the letter which you've sent to me. Therefore I commanded to make search, and it's been found that that city of old time has fought against kings,
\v 27 and the men were given to rebellion and war in it, and that mighty and fierce kings were in Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, who reigned and exacted tribute in Coele-Syria and Foinikai.
\v 28 Now¦107356 therefore¦107357 I've commanded to¦107364 prevent those men from building¦107364 the¦107365 city¦107366, and¦107367 heed to be taken that¦107369 there be nothing¦107370 done contrary to this order,
\v 29 and that those wicked¦107381 doings proceed no further to the¦107383 annoyance of kings¦107384.”
\v 30 Then¦107386 King¦107391 Artahshashta \add !(Artaxerxes)\add*, his letters being read, Rehum, and¦107395 Shimshay the¦107397 scribe¦107398, and¦107399 the¦107400 rest of their associates, went in haste to¦107406 Yerushalem with¦107408 cavalry and a multitude of people in battle array, and began to¦107414 hinder the¦107415 builders. So¦107417 the¦107419 building¦107420 of¦107421 the¦107421 temple¦107422 in¦107424 Yerushalem ceased until¦107426 the¦107427 second¦107428 year of the¦107430 reign of King¦107431 Dareyavesh \add !(Darius)\add* of¦107433 the¦107433 Persians.
\c 3
\s1 Three guards debate what is strongest
\p
\v 1 Now King¦107437 Dareyavesh made¦107439 a great¦107441 feast for all¦107442 his subjects, for all¦107447 who¦107448 were born in his house, for all¦107452 the¦107453 princes of Maday \add !(Media)\add* and¦107457 of Paras \add !(Persia)\add*,
\v 2 and¦107460 for all¦107461 the¦107462 local governors and¦107464 captains and¦107466 governors who¦107468 were under¦107469 him, from¦107471 Hodu to Kush \add !(Ethiopia)\add*, in¦107476 the¦107477 one hundred¦107478 and twenty seven provinces.
\v 3 They ate and¦107481 drank, and¦107483 when¦107485 they were satisfied went home. Then¦107489 King¦107492 Dareyavesh went into¦107499 his bedchamber slept, but¦107500 awakened out of his sleep¦107499.
\p
\v 4 Then the three young men of the bodyguard, who guarded the king, spoke one to another:
\v 5 “Let every one of us state what one thing is strongest. King Dareyavesh will give he whose statement seems wiser than the others great gifts and great honours in token of victory.
\v 6 He'll be clothed in purple¦107548, drink¦107553 from gold¦107556 cups, sleep¦107557 on a gold bed, and¦107558 have a chariot¦107559 with bridles of gold, a fine linen turban, and a chain around¦107566 his neck¦107568.
\v 7 He'll sit next to Dareyavesh because¦107573 of¦107573 his wisdom¦107575, and¦107577 will be called¦107580 cousin of Dareyavesh.”
\p
\v 8 Then they each wrote his sentence, sealed them, and laid them under King Dareyavesh’ pillow,
\v 9 and said, “When the king wakes up, someone will give him the writing. The one who the king and the three princes of Paras \add !(Persia)\add* judge that his sentence is the wisest, to him will the victory be given, as it's written.”
\v 10 The¦107631 first wrote¦107633, “Wine¦107636 is the strongest.”
\v 11 The¦107637 second wrote¦107639, “The¦107641 king¦107642 is strongest.”
\v 12 The¦107643 third¦107644 wrote¦107645, “Women¦107648 are strongest, but above¦107649 all¦107651 things Truth¦107654 is the victor.”
\p
\v 13 Now when¦107656 the¦107658 king¦107659 woke up, they took¦107660 the¦107661 writing¦107662 and gave¦107663 it¦107664 to him, so¦107665 he read it.
\v 14 Sending out, he called¦107669 all¦107670 the¦107671 princes of Paras and¦107675 of Maday \add !(Media)\add*, the¦107676 local governors, the¦107679 captains, the¦107690 governors, and¦107692 the¦107694 chief officers¦107682
\v 15 and¦107698 sat himself¦107708 down in the¦107701 royal seat of judgement; and¦107703 the¦107706 writing was read before them.
\v 16 He¦107715 said¦107714, “Call the¦107719 young men, and they'll explain their own sentences. So they were called and came in.
\s1 The first guard argues for wine
\p
\v 17 They said to them, “Explain what you've written.”
\p Then¦107721 the¦107723 first¦107724, who'd¦107725 spoken of the¦107728 strength¦107729 of¦107730 wine¦107731, began
\v 18 and said this: “Oh sirs, how extremely strong wine is! It causes all men who drink it to go astray.
\v 19 It makes the mind of the king and of the fatherless child to be the same, likewise of the bondman and of the freeman, of the poor man and of the rich.
\v 20 It also¦107775 turns every¦107776 thought into cheer and mirth, so that a man remembers neither sorrow nor debt¦107790.
\v 21 It makes every¦107792 heart¦107793 rich, so¦107796 that a man remembers neither king¦107799 nor local governor. It makes¦107806 people say¦107807 things in large amounts.
\v 22 When¦107811 they're in their cups, they forget their love both to friends¦107814 and¦107815 kindred, and¦107817 before long draw their swords¦107823.
\v 23 But¦107824 when¦107825 they awake from¦107826 their wine¦107828, they don't remember what they've done¦107833.
\v 24 Oh¦107834 sirs, isn't wine¦107839 the strongest, seeing that¦107840 it forces people to¦107843 do¦107843 this?” And¦107844 when he'd said¦107847 this, he stopped speaking.
\c 4
\s1 The second guard argues for the king
\p
\v 1 Then¦107848 the¦107850 second¦107851, who'd spoken¦107852 of¦107858 the¦107858 king's¦107859 strength¦107857, began to¦107854 say¦107854,
\v 2 “Oh¦107860 sirs, don't men¦107861 excel in strength who rule over the¦107869 sea¦107870 and land¦107867, and all¦107873 things in¦107875 them?
\v 3 But yet the king¦107879 is stronger. He's their lord¦107882 and has dominion over¦107882 them. In whatever he commands them, they obey him.
\v 4 If¦107894 he¦107896 tells¦107895 them to¦107897 make¦107897 war¦107898 the one against the¦107901 other¦107902, they do¦107903 it. If¦107904 he¦107907 sends them out against the¦107909 enemies, they go, and¦107912 conquer mountains¦107915, walls¦107918, and¦107919 towers.
\v 5 They kill and¦107923 are killed, and¦107925 don't disobey the king's commandment. If they win the victory, they bring everything to¦107931 the¦107935 king¦107936—all¦107938 the plunder and everything else.
\v 6 Likewise for those who aren't¦107950 soldiers, and¦107946 don't have anything to¦107949 do with wars, but¦107952 farm, when they've reaped again¦107956 that which they had sown, they bring some to the¦107961 king¦107962 and¦107963 compel one another to pay tribute to the¦107971 king¦107972.
\v 7 He's¦107974 just one¦107975 man¦107975! If¦107978 he commands people to kill, they kill. If¦107982 he commands them to spare, they spare.
\v 8 If he commands them to strike, they strike. If he commands them to make desolate, they make desolate. If he commands to build, they build.
\v 9 If he commands them to¦107995 cut down, they cut down. If he commands them to¦107998 plant, they plant.
\v 10 So all his people and his armies obey him. Furthermore, he lies down, he eats and drinks, and takes his rest;
\v 11 and these keep watch around him. None of them may depart and do his own business. They don't disobey him in anything.
\v 12 Oh¦108040 sirs, how¦108042 could the¦108045 king¦108046 not be the strongest, seeing that¦108047 he's¦108049 obeyed¦108049 like this?” Then¦108051 he¦108050 stopped talking.
\s1 Zorobabel argues for women
\p
\v 13 Then the¦108053 third¦108055, who'd spoken of women¦108060, and¦108061 of truth¦108063, (this¦108064 was¦108065 Zorobabel¦108066) began to¦108068 speak¦108068:
\v 14 “Oh sirs, isn't the¦108072 king¦108073 great¦108071, and¦108074 men¦108069 are many, and¦108078 isn't wine¦108080 strong¦108081? Who's it then¦108083 who¦108084 rules them, or¦108087 has the lordship over them? Aren't they women¦108094?
\v 15 Women¦108096 have given birth to¦108097 the¦108098 king¦108099 and¦108100 all¦108101 the¦108102 people¦108103 who rule over¦108105 sea¦108107 and¦108108 land¦108110.
\v 16 They came from¦108112 women. Women nourished up those who¦108119 planted the¦108121 vineyards, from¦108123 where the¦108125 wine¦108126 comes.
\v 17 Women also make clothes for men. These bring glory¦108138 to men. Without¦108146 women¦108148, men can't exist.
\v 18 Yes, and if¦108150 men have gathered together gold¦108153 and¦108154 silver and¦108156 any other beautiful thing¦108158, and¦108160 see a woman¦108162 who's¦108165 lovely in appearance¦108166 and¦108167 beauty,
\v 19 they let all¦108171 those things go and gape at her, and with open mouth¦108179 stare at her. They all¦108183 have more¦108186 desire for her than¦108187 for gold¦108189, or silver, or any other beautiful thing¦108195.
\v 20 A¦108197 man¦108197 leaves his own father¦108200 who¦108206 brought him up, leaves his own¦108207 country¦108208, and¦108209 joins with¦108210 his wife¦108213.
\v 21 With¦108216 his wife¦108218 he ends his days, with no thought for his father¦108225, mother¦108229, or country¦108232.
\v 22 By this also¦108233 you¦108236 must know¦108237 that¦108238 women¦108240 have dominion over¦108241 you¦108242. Don't¦108243 you labour and¦108245 toil, and¦108247 bring¦108253 it all¦108248 to¦108251 give¦108251 to¦108253 women?
\v 23 Yes, a¦108257 man¦108257 takes¦108255 his sword¦108259 and¦108261 goes out to travel, to rob, to¦108267 steal¦108267, and¦108268 to¦108272 sail¦108272 on the¦108270 sea¦108271 and¦108273 on rivers¦108274.
\v 24 He sees¦108278 a lion and¦108279 walks in¦108280 the darkness¦108281. When¦108284 he's stolen¦108285, plundered, and robbed, he brings it to the¦108290 woman he loves.
\v 25 Therefore a¦108296 man¦108298 loves¦108295 his wife¦108299 better than¦108301 father¦108303 or mother¦108306.
\v 26 Yes, there are many who've¦108310 lost their minds for¦108313 women¦108315, and¦108316 become slaves¦108317 for¦108319 their sakes.
\v 27 Many also have perished, have stumbled, and sinned, for¦108328 women¦108330.
\v 28 Now¦108332 don't you believe me? Isn't the¦108338 king¦108339 great¦108337 in his power? Don't¦108343 all¦108344 regions fear to touch him?
\v 29 Yet I saw¦108350 him and¦108352 Apame the king's concubine, the¦108354 daughter¦108355 of¦108357 the illustrious Barticus, sitting¦108363 at the right¦108365 hand of¦108366 the¦108366 king¦108367,
\v 30 and¦108368 taking the¦108370 crown from¦108372 the king's head, and setting it onto her own head. Yes, she struck the¦108382 king¦108383 with her left¦108385 hand.
\v 31 At this¦108388, the¦108389 king¦108390 gaped and gazed at her with open mouth¦108393. If she smiles at him, he laughs. But if she¦108405 takes any displeasure at him, he¦108407 flatters her, that¦108408 she¦108410 might be reconciled to him again.
\v 32 Oh¦108411 sirs, how¦108413 can it not¦108414 be that¦108418 women¦108417 are strong¦108415, seeing they do¦108420 this¦108416?”
\s1 Truth is stronger than everything
\p
\v 33 Then the king and the nobles looked at one another. So he began to speak about truth.
\v 34 “Oh sirs, aren't women strong? The earth is great. The sky is high. The sun is swift in its course, for it circles around the sky, and returns on its course again in one day.
\v 35 Isn't¦108473 he¦108476 who makes¦108477 these things great? Therefore the¦108479 truth¦108480 is great¦108481, and¦108482 stronger than¦108484 all¦108485 things.
\v 36 All¦108486 the¦108487 earth¦108488 calls¦108491 on truth¦108490, and¦108492 the¦108493 sky¦108494 blesses truth. All¦108498 works¦108500 shake and¦108502 tremble, but¦108504 with truth there's no¦108505 unrighteous thing.
\v 37 Wine¦108513 is unrighteous. The¦108515 king¦108516 is unrighteous. Women¦108519 are unrighteous. All¦108521 the¦108522 children¦108523 of men are unrighteous, and¦108526 all¦108528 their works¦108530 are unrighteous. There's no¦108535 truth¦108539 in them. They'll also¦108540 perish in¦108541 their unrighteousness¦108543.
\v 38 But¦108546 truth¦108548 remains, and¦108550 is¦108551 strong¦108551 forever. Truth lives¦108556 and¦108557 conquers forevermore.
\v 39 With truth there's no partiality towards persons or rewards, but truth does the things that are just, instead of any unrighteous or wicked things. All men approve truth's works.
\v 40 In truth's judgement isn't any unrighteousness. Truth is the strength, the kingdom, the power, and the majesty of all ages. Blessed be the god of truth!”
\p
\v 41 With that, he stopped speaking¦108622. Then¦108623 all¦108624 the¦108625 people¦108626 shouted and¦108629 said¦108631, “Great¦108632 is truth¦108634, and¦108635 strong above all things!”
\s1 Zorobabel asks to rebuild Yerushalem
\p
\v 42 Then¦108637 the¦108638 king¦108639 said¦108640 to¦108642 him, “Ask¦108642 what you wish, even more than is appointed in writing¦108647, and¦108648 we'll give¦108649 it to you¦108650, because you're found wisest. You'll sit next me, and will be called my cousin.”
\p
\v 43 Then¦108663 he said¦108664 to¦108664 the¦108665 king¦108666, “Remember¦108667 your vow¦108669, which¦108670 you vowed to¦108672 build¦108672 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, in¦108675 the¦108676 day¦108677 when you¦108681 came to¦108682 your kingdom,
\v 44 and¦108683 to send back all¦108684 the¦108685 vessels¦108686 that¦108687 were taken out¦108689 of Yerushalem, which¦108693 Koresh \add !(Cyrus)\add* set apart when¦108696 he vowed to destroy Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add*, and¦108700 vowed to send them back there¦108703.
\v 45 You¦108705 also¦108704 vowed to¦108707 build¦108707 the¦108708 temple¦108709 which¦108710 the¦108712 Edomim burnt when¦108714 Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add* was made desolate¦108715 by¦108718 the¦108719 Kasdim \add !(Chaldeans)\add*.
\v 46 Now¦108722, Oh lord¦108728 the king¦108729, this is what I request, and what I desire of you¦108733, and¦108734 this¦108735 is¦108736 the¦108737 princely generosity that¦108739 may proceed from¦108740 you¦108741: I ask¦108732 therefore¦108743 that¦108744 you make¦108745 good the¦108746 vow¦108747, the performance of which¦108748 you've vowed to the¦108750 King¦108751 of¦108752 Heaven¦108753 with your own mouth¦108756.”
\p
\v 47 Then¦108758 King¦108762 Dareyavesh \add !(Darius)\add* stood up, kissed¦108763 him, and¦108765 wrote¦108766 letters for him to¦108770 all¦108771 the¦108772 treasurers and¦108774 governors and¦108776 captains and¦108778 local governors, that¦108780 they should safely bring on their way both him, and¦108783 all those who'd¦108784 go up with¦108785 him to¦108789 build¦108789 Yerushalem.
\v 48 He wrote¦108806 letters also¦108792 to all¦108793 the¦108794 governors who were in¦108796 Coele-Syria and¦108799 Foinikai, and¦108801 to them in¦108803 Levanon, that¦108804 they should bring cedar wood¦108809 from¦108811 Levanon to Yerushalem, and¦108816 that¦108817 they should help him build the¦108821 city¦108822.
\v 49 Moreover he wrote¦108824 for all¦108825 the¦108826 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* who'd¦108828 go out of his realm up into Yehudah about their freedom¦108838, that no officer, no governor, no local governor, nor treasurer, should forcibly enter into their doors¦108851,
\v 50 and¦108853 that all¦108854 the¦108855 country¦108856 which¦108857 they occupied should be¦108861 free to them without tribute, and¦108862 that¦108863 the¦108864 Edomim should give up the¦108867 villages¦108868 of the Yehudim which¦108869 they held at that time,
\v 51 and¦108873 that there should be given¦108879 twenty¦108883 talents¦108882 yearly towards¦108874 the¦108875 building¦108876 of¦108877 the¦108877 temple¦108878, until¦108884 the¦108885 time that it was built,
\v 52 and¦108887 another¦108900 ten¦108902 talents¦108901 yearly for burnt¦108891 offerings to¦108888 be presented¦108899 on the¦108889 altar¦108890 every day¦108894, as¦108895 they had a commandment¦108897 to make seventeen¦108898 offerings,
\v 53 and¦108905 that all¦108906 those who'd¦108907 come from¦108909 Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add* to build the¦108913 city¦108914 should have their freedom¦108917—they and their descendants, and all¦108925 the¦108926 priests¦108927 that¦108928 came.
\v 54 He wrote¦108930 also to give them their support and the priests’ vestments in¦108939 which¦108940 they minister¦108941.
\v 55 Because the¦108945 Levites he wrote¦108947 that their support should be given¦108948 them until¦108951 the¦108952 day¦108953 that the¦108956 house¦108957 was finished and¦108958 Yerushalem built up.
\v 56 He commanded that land and¦108961 wages should be given¦108968 to all¦108962 who¦108963 guarded the¦108965 city¦108966.
\v 57 He also sent¦108974 away¦108974 all¦108975 the¦108976 vessels¦108977 from¦108981 Bavel that Koresh \add !(Cyrus)\add* had set apart, and¦108983 all¦108984 that Koresh had given in commandment, he¦108990 commanded¦108991 also to be done¦108988 and¦108989 to¦108991 be sent to¦108992 Yerushalem.
\p
\v 58 Now when¦108998 this¦109000 young¦109001 man¦109001 had gone¦108999 out¦108999, he lifted up¦109002 his face¦109004 to heaven¦109007 towards¦109008 Yerushalem, and praised¦109010 the¦109011 King¦109012 of¦109013 heaven¦109014,
\v 59 and said, “From¦109016 you¦109017 comes victory¦109018. From¦109020 you¦109021 comes wisdom¦109023. Yours is the¦109026 glory¦109027, and¦109028 I'm¦109029 your¦109030 servant¦109031.
\v 60 Blessed are¦109033 you¦109039, who've¦109042 given me wisdom¦109037. I give¦109035 thanks to¦109040 you, Oh master¦109041 of our fathers¦109043.
\v 61 So¦109044 he took¦109045 the¦109046 letters, went¦109049 out¦109049, came to¦109051 Bavel, and¦109054 told it¦109058 all¦109059 his kindred.
\v 62 They praised the¦109062 god¦109063 of their ancestors, because he'd¦109066 given¦109068 them freedom and¦109071 liberty
\v 63 to go up and¦109074 to¦109075 build¦109075 Yerushalem and¦109078 the¦109079 temple¦109080 which¦109081 is called¦109082 by his name¦109084. They feasted with¦109090 instruments of music and¦109092 gladness¦109093 seven¦109095 days¦109094.
\c 5
\s1 The families returning from exile
\p
\v 1 After¦109096 this¦109098, the chiefs of fathers’ houses¦109102 were chosen to go up according¦109104 to¦109104 their tribes¦109105, with their wives, sons¦109113, and¦109115 daughters¦109117, with their male servants and female servants, and their livestock.
\v 2 Dareyavesh \add !(Darius)\add* sent with¦109132 them one thousand cavalry to bring them back to Yerushalem with¦109142 peace¦109143, with¦109145 musical instruments, drums, and¦109148 flutes.
\v 3 All¦109151 their kindred were making merry, and¦109156 he¦109158 made them go up together with¦109160 them.
\p
\v 4 These are the¦109164 names¦109165 of the¦109166 men¦109167 who¦109168 went up, according¦109170 to¦109170 their families¦109171 among their tribes¦109175, after their several divisions.
\v 5 The¦109180 priests¦109181, the sons of Pinhas, the¦109187 sons of¦109188 Aharon \add !(Aaron)\add*: Yeshua \add !(Jesus)\add* the¦109188 son of¦109190 Yotsadak, the¦109190 son of Serayah, and¦109192 Yoyakim the¦109194 son of¦109195 Zorobabel¦109196, the son of Salathiail, of the¦109200 house¦109201 of¦109202 David, of the¦109202 lineage of Perets, of the¦109205 tribe¦109208 of Yehudah \add !(Judah)\add*,
\v 6 who spoke¦109212 wise¦109219 words¦109218 before Dareyavesh the¦109215 king¦109216 of Paras \add !(Persia)\add* in¦109220 the¦109221 second¦109222 year¦109223 of his reign, in the¦109224 month¦109227 Nisan¦109228, which¦109211 is the¦109229 first¦109230 month.
\p
